ASAN:125: #  AB_EXEMPT -- THE KNOWN-DEAD SET, DATED AND NAMED.
ASAN:126: #
ASAN:127: #  Same idiom as gate_offline.sh's SM_EXEMPT/SC_EXEMPT, and for the same reason: a gate
ASAN:128: #  that carries debt must carry it OUT LOUD. Entries are `type/subtype:YYYY-MM-DD`.
ASAN:129: #
ASAN:130: #  WHY A LIST AND NOT A FLOOR. A floor cannot express "none of them died" -- that was
ASAN:131: #  B1's second half. A hard count could, but it is brittle: it breaks every time the
ASAN:132: #  machine list changes, for no defect. A NAMED list is neither. Adding a machine that
ASAN:133: #  works changes nothing; a machine that starts dying is red the same day; and a machine
ASAN:134: #  that stops dying is ALSO red (see the "no longer die" check), so the list cannot rot
ASAN:135: #  into a lie the way SM_COVERED did before it was cross-checked against real work.
ASAN:136: #
ASAN:137: #  WHY THESE FIFTEEN, AND WHY THE DIRECTIONAL FRAME DOES NOT COVER THEM.
ASAN:138: #  All fifteen were MEASURED on both binaries: they abort IDENTICALLY under pristine
ASAN:139: #  39748e3 and under HEAD (rc=134, SIGABRT, same reason text). So the directional rule --
ASAN:140: #  the one at the top of this file -- classifies every one of them as "pre-existing,
ASAN:141: #  reported not failed", and a purely directional gate would print them and pass. That is
ASAN:142: #  right for attributing blame and WRONG for knowing what is dead, which is why this gate
ASAN:143: #  now asserts BOTH: `died on HEAD but constructs upstream` (directional, blame) and
ASAN:144: #  `died and is not on this list` (absolute, inventory).
ASAN:145: #
ASAN:146: #  They are upstream's own unported-legacy markers, not fork damage. THREE shapes, each
ASAN:147: #  read at the cited line rather than guessed, and the table's `reason` column prints
ASAN:148: #  which one a given row hit:
